- `scslide-py` is NOT an official package or endorsed distribution from
  Satija Lab or the New York Genome Center.

1. Upstream Methodological and API Specifications (Clean-Room Native Port)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The 19 public capabilities (`F01`–`F19`), mathematical contracts, parameter
semantics, and reference profiles (`v6` and `legacy`) correspond to the
published open-source R implementations below, both distributed under the MIT
License:

- `scSLIDE` (R package, v0.1.1, archived upstream reference `968dbde2`):
  Copyright (c) 2024 scSLIDE authors (Longda Jiang, Satija Lab).
  URL: https://github.com/satijalab/scSLIDE (MIT License).
- `Seurat` (R package, v6.0.0 upstream reference `e6ba2b24`):
  Copyright (c) Satija Lab and Collaborators (Rahul Satija, Longda Jiang,
  Yuhan Hao, Tim Stuart, Andrew Butler, Paul Hoffman, et al.).
  URL: https://github.com/satijalab/seurat (MIT License).

All Python modules under `src/scslide/` are written natively in Python over
NumPy, SciPy, pandas, scikit-learn, and matplotlib without bundling or linking
upstream R or C++ source files and without calling `Rscript` or `rpy2` at
runtime.

2. Published Statistical Formulations Implemented Natively
----------------------------------------------------------
- Weighted Nearest Neighbor (WNN) & Sketching: Hao et al. (2021), Cell;
  Hao et al. (2023), Nature Biotechnology.
- Diffusion Maps (`destiny` locally scaled anisotropic Markov operator):
  Haghverdi, Buettner & Theis (2015), Bioinformatics; Angerer et al. (2016),
  Bioinformatics.
- Principal Curves (`princurve` iterative polyline projection & Reinsch cubic
  smoothing spline): Hastie & Stuetzle (1989), JASA; Green & Silverman (1994).
- Gamma-Poisson Quasi-Likelihood GLM (`glmGamPoi` Cox-Reid profile likelihood
  and empirical-Bayes dispersion shrinkage): Ahlmann-Eltze & Huber (2020),
  Bioinformatics.
- Partial Least Squares (`kernelpls`, `cppls`, `spls`): Dayal & MacGregor
  (1997), J. Chemometrics; Indahl, Liland & Naes (2009), J. Chemometrics;
  Chun & Keles (2010), JRSS-B.
- Control-Referenced Batch Adjustment (`ComBat` & `CellANOVA`): Johnson, Li &
  Rabinovic (2007), Biostatistics; Zhang et al. (2024), Nature Biotechnology.

3. Runtime and Optional Third-Party Dependencies (Not Bundled)
--------------------------------------------------------------
`scslide-py` declares runtime and optional dependencies in `pyproject.toml`
and invokes them via their public Python APIs (`numpy`, `pandas`, `scipy`,
`scikit-learn`, `matplotlib`; optional `anndata`, `h5py`, `zarr`,
`pynndescent`, `umap-learn`, `annoy`). None of those third-party libraries are
vendored inside `scslide-py` source or binary distributions.

4. Datasets and Non-Redistribution Policy
-----------------------------------------
No patient-level, donor-level, or clinical biological datasets are included in
`scslide-py` wheel or sdist distributions. Bundled onboarding fixtures
(`scslide.build_synthetic_cohort_fixture`, `scslide-calibrate`, and
`tests/fixtures/lmc_contract.json`) are purely synthetic and MIT-licensed.
Retrospective case evaluations referenced in repository documentation used
publicly governed datasets (COMBAT Consortium / Ahern et al., 2022, Cell;
SEA-AD Consortium MTG Microglia-PVM) locally without redistributing raw or
processed human count matrices inside distributable package artifacts.
